        "original_id": "8bb9fad1-7402-5668-a062-a2695c1a53c4",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: John Wesley, Charles Wesley, George Whitefield, Edward Perronet\n- Organizations: Methodism, Church of England, St Peter's, Eastern Hill\n- Places: England, Melbourne, Victoria, Australia\n- Roles: Anglican cleric, theologian, Anglican priest, parish church\n- Concepts: 18th-century revival\n\nFacts:\n- Methodism originated as a revival within the 18th-century Church of England and became a separate denomination after John Wesley's death.\n- John Wesley, born on 28 June 1703 and died on 2 March 1791, was an English Anglican cleric and theologian.\n- John Wesley, his brother Charles Wesley, and fellow cleric George Whitefield founded Methodism.\n- Edward Perronet, born in 1726 and died on 2 January 1792, was the son of an Anglican priest.\n- Edward Perronet worked closely with Anglican priest John Wesley and Charles Wesley for many years during England's 18th-century revival.\n- St Peter's, Eastern Hill is the Anglican parish church of the City of Melbourne in Victoria, Australia.",
